id=a52sxq_usb_webcam
name=Galaxy A52s 5G: use the phone as a USB webcam
author=kusti420
description=One UI 9 ships Android's DeviceAsWebcam (USB options: "Webcam", USB function "uvc"); the S26 Ultra turns it on with the vendor prop ro.usb.uvc.enabled=true, a UVC gadget in its vendor init.qcom.usb.rc and a kernel with the configfs UVC function. The A52s has none of the three: its A14 vendor only carries Qualcomm's old CAF uvc block in init.qcom.usb.sh (720p frames written with the width twice, an h264 group the 5.4 driver doesn't know), no "uvc" / "uvc,adb" USB configurations, and the bone-machine defconfig leaves CONFIG_USB_CONFIGFS_F_UVC off. The framework side is complete (SecSettings mUVCEnabled, UsbService.isUvcGadgetSupportEnabled, plat_sepolicy device_as_webcam with video_device access, /dev/video* is video_device 0660 system camera). This patch adds the vendor half: the CAF block is replaced by MJPEG descriptors taken from the S26 Ultra's but cut down to 640x480, 1280x720 and 1920x1080 at 30/24/15 fps (the S26U also offers 640x360 and 60 fps: the A52s camera HAL rejects 640x360, which hung DeviceAsWebcam, and cannot feed 60 fps MJPEG; no h264 or uncompressed, as on the S26U), the uvc and uvc,adb configurations are added with the S26U's Samsung ids (04E8:68CF), /dev/video2-9 (where the gadget node lands) are created with group system so DeviceAsWebcam (uid system, no camera gid) can open the node the moment it appears, and ro.usb.uvc.enabled is set - but only when the kernel in work_dir/kernel/boot.img carries the UVC gadget (configfs attribute "streaming_maxpacket"), so a ROM built with a kernel that lacks it never offers a Webcam option that can't work. The kernel half is kernel/0009-usb-gadget-uvc-webcam-function-and-format-enumeration.patch for ~/git/kernel_a52sxq_bone/k4-patches: it enables the function and backports VIDIOC_ENUM_FMT / ENUM_FRAMESIZES / ENUM_FRAMEINTERVALS (DeviceAsWebcam enumerates the gadget's formats with them; 5.4 has none, upstream added them in 6.1), enumerating the bound streaming descriptors. USB 2.0 high speed: isochronous 3x1024 bytes per microframe (about 24 MB/s), plenty for 1080p30 MJPEG.
